Chapter 2 – Background and Related Work

This chapter situates SAOA within the landscape of multi‑agent scientific discovery, reinforcement learning, Bayesian modeling, knowledge representation, neuro‑symbolic verification, credit assignment, and knowledge distillation. Because the field is evolving rapidly and many systems are described only in preprints or workshop papers, the discussion emphasises foundational concepts and established results. Where reference is made to concurrent architectures, the descriptions are kept general, and all factual claims about third‑party systems will be verified against their actual publications before the final submission of this thesis.

2.1 Multi‑Agent Systems for Scientific Discovery

The pursuit of autonomous scientific discovery has a long history. Early closed‑loop systems such as the Robot Scientist “Adam” (King et al., 2004) combined laboratory automation with logic‑based hypothesis generation, but their control logic was entirely hand‑crafted and domain‑specific. The recent explosion of large language models (LLMs) has opened the door to more flexible agents that can reason in natural language, retrieve information, and invoke computational tools. For example, ChemCrow (Bran et al., 2024) augmented a single LLM with chemistry‑specific tools and demonstrated that such an agent could tackle a variety of tasks, albeit within a single‑agent, non‑collaborative framework.

Multi‑agent architectures promise to extend these capabilities by distributing expertise across several specialised agents that communicate and share a common knowledge base. In the materials science domain, several groups have explored LLM‑based agents that query databases such as the Materials Project and perform simulations, but they typically use fixed routing or heuristic task assignment. Hierarchical structures, where a planning agent decomposes a high‑level goal into subtasks that are then dispatched to specialised agents, are emerging as a common pattern. However, to the best of our knowledge, no existing framework formalises the task assignment problem as a full Markov decision process with Bayesian capability tracking at the per‑concept level, nor do they incorporate the full suite of verification, conflict resolution, credit assignment, and knowledge distillation mechanisms that SAOA provides.

2.2 Reinforcement Learning and MDP Orchestration

Framing the coordination of multiple agents as a Markov decision process is a well‑grounded approach. In the most general formulation, the orchestration problem can be cast as a Dec‑POMDP, but when the orchestrator has a centralised view of the state (knowledge graph, task DAG, and agent capabilities), a single‑agent MDP often suffices. The state space in SAOA is rich: it includes the evolving knowledge graph, the dependency structure of pending tasks, and an information‑theoretic measure of uncertainty. Most existing RL‑based orchestrators operate on much coarser state representations, such as agent availability or simple performance histories.

Model‑based planning with Monte‑Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) is a natural choice for such large state spaces, provided the state can be represented compactly. Recent work on graph neural networks as state encoders has shown that GNNs can compress relational knowledge into fixed‑size embeddings that are amenable to policy learning. SAOA adopts this strategy by passing the Unified Scientific Ontology graph through a Graph Attention Network before feeding it to the planner.

2.3 Bayesian Capability Modeling and Thompson Sampling

Tracking each agent’s success probability on a task type via a Beta‑Bernoulli conjugate model is the canonical choice when outcomes are binary (success / failure). The posterior parameters, (\alpha, \beta), compactly summarise the agent’s history and yield a well‑calibrated uncertainty estimate. Thompson sampling—drawing a random success probability from the posterior and selecting the agent with the highest draw—is known to achieve logarithmic regret under the right conditions (Agrawal & Goyal, 2012).

SAOA refines this idea in two ways. First, capability is modelled per scientific concept, not per agent as a whole, so that an agent strong in band‑gap prediction but weak in synthesis design is assigned tasks accordingly. Second, because tasks in a scientific workflow are rarely independent, Thompson sampling is extended to a coordination graph that encodes pairwise dependencies. The literature on factored multi‑agent bandits (e.g., Verstraeten et al., 2020) demonstrates that sampling over a sparse graph can preserve sublinear regret, a result that SAOA inherits by construction.

2.4 Knowledge Graphs and Scientific Ontologies

The representation of scientific knowledge in machine‑readable form has advanced considerably. The Open Research Knowledge Graph (ORKG) and domain‑specific resources like the Materials Project database provide structured, curated data. Upper‑level ontologies, such as the Modern Science Ontology (ModSci) within the Science Knowledge Graph Ontology suite, define foundational classes (Material, Process, Property, Experiment) and relations that enable semantic interoperability.

Multi‑agent systems that collaboratively populate a knowledge graph face the challenge of conflicting information. Prior work on collaborative knowledge‑graph enrichment has used heuristic voting to resolve contradictions, but these methods lack a formal probabilistic foundation. SAOA instead anchors its Knowledge Graph in a probabilistic framework: each hypothesis node carries not only a claim but a posterior belief derived from agent capability, and conflicts are resolved through a Bayesian protocol that respects both quantitative uncertainty and structural context.

2.5 Neuro‑Symbolic Verification

A growing body of research advocates for hybrid neuro‑symbolic architectures to ensure the safety and correctness of AI‑generated scientific claims. The PEIRCE system (Quan et al., ACL 2025) uses a combination of symbolic provers and soft evaluators to iteratively critique and refine hypotheses. Other work has framed scientific verification as a Constraint Satisfaction Problem, using lightweight symbolic gates to reject physically impossible proposals before they reach a human reviewer.

SAOA builds a two‑tier gate that makes the symbolic check entirely deterministic and couples it with a learned critic. The novelty lies in the structure of the neural critic: it is implemented as a mixture‑of‑experts, with separate heads trained on retraction corpora (for macroscopic fraud and statistics errors), scientific claim‑verification datasets (for evidence‑based refutation), and synthetically generated physical‑constraint violations (for domain‑specific plausibility). This design covers a broad spectrum of error types that a single‑head model might miss.

2.6 Credit Assignment in Cooperative AI

Fairly distributing reward among cooperating agents is essential for sustained collective learning. The Shapley value (Shapley, 1953) is the unique allocation scheme that satisfies several natural fairness axioms, including the dummy player property: an agent that contributes nothing to any coalition receives zero reward. This property directly eliminates free‑riding equilibria.

Applying Shapley values to multi‑agent systems requires defining the utility of a coalition. Early work in multi‑agent reinforcement learning often resorted to counterfactual re‑execution, which is computationally prohibitive. More recent approaches compute Shapley values at the level of individual generated outputs (e.g., sentences or reasoning steps). SAOA adopts a process‑level attribution: every verified node in the knowledge graph carries a provenance trace that identifies the agents that contributed to it, and the coalition utility is simply the sum of quality scores of the nodes contributed by that coalition. This makes the computation efficient, log‑based, and provably anti‑free‑riding through Lemma 1 in Chapter 4.

2.7 Knowledge Distillation Across Agents

Knowledge distillation, originally proposed by Hinton et al. (2015) as a way to transfer knowledge from a large teacher model to a smaller student, has been extended to multi‑agent settings. Recent work has explored distilling the collective knowledge of a group of agents into a single compact model, or using parameter‑efficient fine‑tuning techniques such as LoRA to transfer expertise between agents without retraining the entire backbone.

SAOA operationalises this idea through a trigger‑conditioned distillation process: when one agent becomes highly competent on a concept (\mu > 0.9) and another lags behind (\mu < 0.6), the system attempts transfer. The critical addition is a suite of safeguarding mechanisms—a distributional compatibility check and an exponential‑backoff blacklist—that prevent the repeated, costly failures that can arise when two agents’ internal representations are fundamentally misaligned.

2.8 Differentiating SAOA from Prior Work

Rather than risk inaccuracies by comparing SAOA point‑by‑point with preprints whose details may not be final, we characterise SAOA by the unique combination of features it integrates:

· Orchestration is formalised as an MDP over a GNN‑compressed state that includes the full knowledge graph, task DAG, and global uncertainty.
· Capability is modelled per concept and per agent via Bayesian updating and tracked over long horizons.
· Task assignment is performed by factored Thompson sampling over a coordination graph that captures inter‑agent dependencies, with a proven regret bound.
· Conflicts are resolved through a rigorous distance‑based protocol that adjudicates between Bayesian merging and simulation‑backed arbitration.
· A two‑tier neuro‑symbolic verification gate, with a mixture‑of‑experts critic, rejects unsafe or impossible claims before they enter the knowledge base.
· Credit assignment uses process‑level Shapley values, anchored to verified nodes, and comes with an explicit anti‑free‑riding guarantee.
· Knowledge distillation is safeguarded by a distributional compatibility score and a blacklisting mechanism.
· The meta‑cognitive layer is decoupled from the orchestrator via feudal reinforcement learning, preventing training instabilities.
· A mandatory benchmark snapshot and LLM‑caching protocol ensure that all results are reproducible, even across hardware and software changes.

To the best of our knowledge, no prior architecture combines all of these elements in a single, mathematically grounded framework.

