Chapter 3 – The SAOA Architecture

3.1 System Overview

SAOA is organised as a layered stack of six subsystems, each communicating through well‑defined interfaces. Figure 3.1 (conceptual) illustrates the data and control flow.

```
+-------------------+
| Meta‑Cognitive    |  Feudal RSA: goal vectors, strategy proposals
+-------------------+
        |
+-------------------+
| Orchestration     |  MDP planner + GNN state encoder
+-------------------+
     |        |
+-------------------+   +-------------------+
| Domain Expertise  |   | Grounding         |
| (Materials, Chem, |   | (Verification)    |
|  Physics, Stats)  |   |                   |
+-------------------+   +-------------------+
     |        |                |
+-------------------+   +-------------------+
| Learning          |   | Communication     |
| (Distillation)    |   | (USO, SCI‑ACP)    |
+-------------------+   +-------------------+
```

The Meta‑Cognitive Layer contains the Research Strategy Agent (RSA), which operates on a slow timescale and issues goal vectors to guide the system’s exploration–exploitation balance. The Orchestration Layer is an MDP‑based module that maintains a global state (knowledge graph, task DAG, uncertainty), compresses it via a Graph Attention Network, and selects actions using Monte‑Carlo Tree Search and factored Thompson sampling. Domain Expertise agents are heterogeneous specialists that wrap LLMs, tools, and retrieval‑augmented generation. The Grounding Layer enforces physical and methodological soundness through a two‑tier verification gate. The Learning Layer enables cross‑agent knowledge transfer with safeguards. Finally, the Communication Layer ensures semantic interoperability through the Unified Scientific Ontology and the SCI‑ACP message protocol.

3.2 Meta‑Cognitive Layer

The RSA is a high‑level feudal policy. Every N_{\text{rsa}} = 50 orchestrator steps, it observes the cumulative reward and outputs a goal vector g \in \mathbb{R}^d that encodes the desired direction for knowledge‑graph evolution (e.g., “increase diversity of Material candidates”, “reduce uncertainty around band‑gap predictions”). The orchestrator, during its lower‑level steps, receives an intrinsic penalty proportional to \| \Delta \text{state} - g \|^2, which pushes it to align its actions with the RSA’s intent. The RSA is trained using the temporally extended reward, while the orchestrator’s policy is updated on its own faster timescale with g held fixed. This feudal separation eliminates the circular gradient dependency that would otherwise destabilise joint training.

The RSA also monitors the global uncertainty \Omega_t. When \Omega_t plateaus, the RSA can propose switching to an exploration mode, encouraging the orchestrator to sample agents with wide Beta distributions or to invoke generative models that produce novel hypotheses.

3.3 Orchestration Layer

The orchestration layer is the central decision‑maker. Its state at time t is a tuple s_t = (G_t, \Phi_t, \Omega_t, H_t), where

· G_t is the current USO knowledge graph,
· \Phi_t is the pending task directed acyclic graph,
· \Omega_t is the global Shannon entropy over unresolved hypotheses,
· H_t is a truncated history of SCI‑ACP messages.

To avoid the curse of dimensionality, the graph G_t is processed by a Graph Attention Network (GAT). Node features include the type of entity (Material, Process, etc.), the current posterior belief of any hypothesis, and the uncertainty of its value. Edge features encode the USO relation type. The GAT produces a fixed‑size embedding h_t \in \mathbb{R}^{256} that summarises the relational knowledge. This embedding, together with vectorized representations of \Phi_t, \Omega_t, and H_t, forms the input to the MCTS planner.

The action space includes five types: assignment of a task to an agent, verification of a proposed node, acceptance or modification of an RSA strategy, distillation between agents, and arbitration to resolve a conflict. The planner uses a depth‑3 lookahead with progressive widening, and the policy network is trained on the GNN‑encoded state to maximise expected cumulative information gain, penalised by computational cost and augmented with a goal‑achievement term (Section 4.7).

Task assignment within the orchestrator uses factored Thompson sampling. Every agent i has, for each concept k, a Beta posterior (\alpha_{i,k}, \beta_{i,k}). A raw competence sample \hat{c}_{i,k} is drawn, and an additive penalty based on the weakest upstream agent is applied to give the effective competence \tilde{c}_{i,k}. The agent with the highest effective competence minus cost is selected. Full mathematical details appear in Section 4.2.

3.4 Domain Expertise Layer

Each domain agent is an autonomous specialist. Internally, it consists of an LLM backbone (fine‑tuned for the domain), a set of domain‑specific tools (database query interfaces, simulation wrappers, cheminformatics libraries), and a local RAG index. Agents communicate with the rest of the system solely through the SCI‑ACP protocol and by proposing nodes to the USO graph. The orchestrator is agnostic to their internals; it only observes their Beta capability posteriors and the verification outcomes of their proposals.

The Materials Agent, described in detail in Section 5.3, illustrates the pattern. It combines a 13B‑parameter LLM with retrieval over the Materials Project, AFLOW, and OQMD databases, and can invoke DFT calculations or generative models when no database match exists. All claims are tagged with a confidence level that serves as a prior for the Bayesian capability update.

3.5 Grounding Layer

Every proposed addition to the knowledge graph must pass two checks before acceptance.

The first is the Symbolic Hard Auditor. It enforces dimensional homogeneity (via the pint library), conservation laws (mass, charge, energy, momentum), chemical valence and bonding rules (via RDKit), and Buckingham \pi theorem compliance. Any failure results in immediate, deterministic rejection with a detailed log.

The second is the Neural Critic, implemented as a mixture‑of‑experts. A lightweight router examines the proposal’s domain tag and metadata and activates one or more of three specialist heads:

1. Retraction head: Trained on a corpus of retracted papers and negative peer reviews, it detects macroscopic flaws, statistical fabrication, and methodological errors.
2. Claim‑verification head: Trained on SciFact, SCIVER, and SciClaimEval, it assesses whether the claim is supported by cited evidence.
3. Physical‑constraint head: Trained on synthetically generated violations of known physical laws (wrong units, broken conservation), it catches subtle domain‑specific impossibilities.

The critic outputs a probability p_{\text{critic}} that the proposal is flawed. The acceptance threshold \tau is adapted by the RSA: lower (\approx 0.8) for exploratory phases, higher (\approx 0.99) for safety‑critical tasks. Rejection decrements the originating agent’s Beta success counts and flags the subtask for revision.

3.6 Learning Layer

The Knowledge Distillation module is triggered when the orchestrator detects a large pairwise competence gap on the same concept: source agent i has posterior mean \mu_{i,k} > 0.9, target agent j has \mu_{j,k} < 0.6. Before attempting transfer, the system computes a distributional compatibility score \kappa(i,j,k) by drawing multiple random subsets of concept‑k instances, passing them through both agents’ encoders, and averaging the cosine similarity of the output embeddings. If the mean similarity is too low or its variance too high, neural distillation is blocked and the system defaults to symbolic rule extraction.

If neural transfer is permitted, the source agent generates a distilled dataset (input–output pairs) for concept k, and the target agent performs a lightweight LoRA fine‑tuning on that dataset. The improvement is evaluated on a held‑out set; only if accuracy improves are the target’s \alpha_{j,k} counts incremented. If no improvement occurs (negative transfer), the LoRA adapter is rolled back, and the failure is recorded. An exponential‑backoff blacklist prevents immediate retries: after one failure, 10 task cycles must pass before another attempt; after two, 100 cycles; after three, the pair (i,j) is permanently blacklisted for concept k and future distillation is routed through a bridge agent if possible.

3.7 Communication Layer

The USO provides a canonical vocabulary and a labeled property graph schema. Constraints are enforced programmatically by the symbolic auditor rather than by an OWL reasoner, avoiding the architectural contradiction of claiming OWL 2 DL alignment while using a Neo4j backend. The USO’s alignment with the ModSci upper ontology is conceptual, ensuring export compatibility with institutional knowledge graphs.

The Science Community Inter‑Agent Communication Protocol (SCI‑ACP) defines 11 structured JSON message types covering queries, hypotheses, evidence, refutations, simulation requests, conflict notifications, arbitration verdicts, strategy proposals, and distillation instructions. All messages are timestamped, signed with agent identity, and exchanged via a publish‑subscribe message bus. The protocol is designed to map onto emerging standards such as the Model Context Protocol, enabling future inter‑institutional federation.

