Chapter 4 – Mathematical Foundations

This chapter provides the formal mathematical specification of SAOA’s core components: the orchestration MDP, factored Thompson sampling, process‑level Shapley credit assignment, verification logic, conflict resolution with the USO distance metric, knowledge distillation safeguards, and the feudal RL decoupling of the meta‑cognitive layer.

4.1 Orchestration MDP

SAOA’s orchestration problem is a finite‑horizon Markov decision process \mathcal{M} = \langle S, A_{\text{orch}}, P, R, \gamma \rangle, where:

· S is the state space. A state s_t is a tuple (G_t, \Phi_t, \Omega_t, H_t). In practice, the policy network receives the GAT embedding h_t = \text{GAT}(G_t) concatenated with feature vectors for \Phi_t, \Omega_t, H_t.
· A_{\text{orch}} contains five action types: assignment, verification, strategy modification, distillation, and arbitration.
· P is the transition function, which we model as being biased toward uncertainty reduction: P(s_{t+1}|s_t,a_t) \propto \exp(\alpha \cdot \Delta I), where \Delta I is the expected reduction in graph entropy.
· R is the reward function,
  R(s_t,a_t,s_{t+1}) = \beta \cdot \Delta I - \lambda \cdot \text{cost}(a_t) + \mathbf{1}_{\text{solution valid}} \cdot R_{\text{final}},
  with \beta, \lambda \ge 0 and \gamma \in (0,1).

The policy is optimised via Monte‑Carlo Tree Search with depth‑3 lookahead, progressive widening, and cached state values for common subgraphs.

4.2 Factored Thompson Sampling with Additive Penalty

For each agent i \in \mathcal{A} and scientific concept k \in \mathcal{C}, we maintain

\theta_{i,k} \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha_{i,k}, \beta_{i,k}),

updated via the standard conjugate rule: \alpha \leftarrow \alpha + v, \beta \leftarrow \beta + (1-v) for a verified success (v=1) or failure (v=0).

Coordination graph. From the task DAG \Phi_t, we construct a directed graph C = (\mathcal{A}, E_C) where (p,i) \in E_C if the output of agent p is a direct input to agent i for the current task. Let \text{up}(i) be the set of upstream agents for i.

Effective competence. Sample a raw competence \hat{c}_{i,k} \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha_{i,k}, \beta_{i,k}). The effective competence is

\tilde{c}_{i,k} = \hat{c}_{i,k} - \lambda_c \cdot \bigl(1 - \min_{p \in \text{up}(i)} \mu_{p,k}\bigr),

where \mu_{p,k} = \alpha_{p,k}/(\alpha_{p,k}+\beta_{p,k}) is the posterior mean. The orchestrator selects the agent maximising \tilde{c}_{i,k} - \lambda \cdot \text{cost}(i).

Why additive. A multiplicative product \tilde{c} = \hat{c} \prod \mu_{p} would geometrically suppress agents deep in a workflow, biasing the system toward short chains regardless of actual downstream capability. The additive penalty harms the score only to the extent of the weakest upstream agent, preserving the exploration–exploitation balance.

Regret bound. When C is sparse (max degree d \ll n), the assignment rule falls under the factored bandit framework of Verstraeten et al. (2020). Their Theorem 1 applies directly, yielding expected cumulative regret O(\sqrt{T} \cdot \text{poly}(d, K)) over T steps and K concepts. The deterministic penalty term acts as a fixed scaling, so the bound carries over without modification.

4.3 Process‑Level Shapley Value Credit Assignment

When an episode yields a verified discovery with total reward R, we distribute credit according to the Shapley value defined over the set of verified nodes \mathcal{V}.

Coalition utility. For S \subseteq \mathcal{A}, let \mathcal{V}_S be the nodes whose provenance trace shows that all contributing agents belong to S. Each node v has a quality score q(v) \in [0,1] determined by the verification layer. Then

v(S) = \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}_S} q(v).

This definition is computable directly from the run logs without counterfactual re‑execution.

Shapley value. For agent i,

\phi_i = \frac{1}{n!} \sum_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{A}\setminus\{i\}} |\mathcal{S}|!\,(n-|\mathcal{S}|-1)! \bigl[v(\mathcal{S} \cup \{i\}) - v(\mathcal{S})\bigr].

In practice we estimate \phi_i via m = 100 random permutations and average the marginal contributions.

Lemma 1 (Anti‑Free‑Riding). If an agent contributes no verified nodes—i.e., \mathcal{V}_{S \cup \{i\}} = \mathcal{V}_S for every S \subseteq \mathcal{A}\setminus\{i\}—then v(S \cup \{i\}) = v(S) for all S, and consequently \phi_i = 0.

Proof. Under the condition, every marginal contribution is zero. By the dummy player axiom of the Shapley value, \phi_i = 0. ∎

4.4 Verification Logic

A proposed node C is accepted into G_t iff

V(C) = P_{\text{symbolic}}(C) \wedge (p_{\text{critic}} > \tau),

where P_{\text{symbolic}}(C) is the output of the deterministic symbolic auditor (1 if all constraints satisfied, else 0) and p_{\text{critic}} is the neural critic’s probability that C is invalid. The threshold \tau is set adaptively by the RSA.

4.5 Conflict Resolution Protocol with USO Distance

When two nodes v_i, v_j assert contradictory values for the same property, the CRP proceeds as follows.

Step 1 – Distance computation. The USO distance is

\delta(v_i, v_j) = w_q \cdot \frac{|x_i - x_j|}{\sigma_i + \sigma_j} + w_s \cdot d_{\text{graph}}(v_i, v_j),

where x_i, x_j are the quantitative claims (unit‑normalised), \sigma_i = \sqrt{\alpha_i\beta_i / [(\alpha_i+\beta_i)^2(\alpha_i+\beta_i+1)]} is the Beta posterior standard deviation, and d_{\text{graph}} is the graph‑edit distance between the local USO subgraphs rooted at the two nodes. The weights w_q, w_s are tuned on held‑out conflict data.

Step 2 – Decision. If \delta < 1, the conflict is low‑level; the two nodes are merged via Bayesian precision‑weighted averaging, and their uncertainties are combined in quadrature. If \delta \ge 1, the conflict escalates to Deep Arbitration: a referee agent with the highest Beta mean on the concept is selected, or, if none exists, a Shared World Model simulation (DFT, MD, etc.) is invoked to establish the ground truth. Both agents then receive capability updates based on the verdict.

4.6 Knowledge Distillation with Safeguards

Trigger. Distillation from agent i to agent j on concept k is initiated when \mu_{i,k} > \theta_{\text{high}} and \mu_{j,k} < \theta_{\text{low}} (e.g., 0.9 and 0.6).

Distributional compatibility. Let D_k be the set of prototypical instances for concept k. Draw m=10 random subsets D' \subset D_k of size \min(20, |D_k|/2). Compute

\kappa(i,j,k) = \mathbb{E}_{D'}\bigl[\cos\bigl(f_{\text{enc}}^{(i)}(D'), f_{\text{enc}}^{(j)}(D')\bigr)\bigr],

and record its variance \text{Var}[\cos]. If \kappa < \theta_{\text{rep}} or \text{Var}[\cos] > \sigma^2_{\max}, neural distillation is blocked; the system falls back to symbolic rule transfer.

Transfer. If neural distillation proceeds, the target agent j undergoes LoRA fine‑tuning on the dataset generated by i. Accuracy is evaluated on a held‑out test set T_k. If \text{acc}_{\text{after}} > \text{acc}_{\text{before}}, the transfer is successful and \alpha_{j,k} is incremented proportionally. Otherwise, the LoRA adapter is rolled back, and the failure is recorded.

Blacklist. A counter r_{i,j,k} tracks consecutive failures. After the first failure, retry is barred for 10^{r} task cycles (r=1,\dots,3). After three failures, the pair (i,j) is permanently blacklisted for concept k.

4.7 Feudal Decoupling of RSA and Orchestrator

The RSA and the orchestrator operate on different timescales. Every N_{\text{rsa}} = 50 orchestrator steps, the RSA receives the cumulative reward R_{\text{rsa}} = \sum_{t'=t}^{t+N-1} R_{t'} and outputs a goal vector g \in \mathbb{R}^d. During each low‑level step, the orchestrator’s reward is

R^{\text{orch}}_t = R^{\text{MDP}}_t - \eta \cdot \| \Delta \text{state}_{\text{USO}} - g \|^2,

where \Delta \text{state}_{\text{USO}} is the change in the GAT embedding of the USO graph. The penalty term is minimised when the graph evolution matches the goal, correctly incentivising alignment. The RSA is updated by a policy‑gradient method on the temporally extended return, while the orchestrator is updated with g treated as constant. This feudal structure prevents the non‑stationarity that would arise from jointly training two coupled policies on the same reward signal.

Chapter 5 – Technical Specifications

This chapter translates the architectural and mathematical foundations laid out in Chapters 3 and 4 into concrete technical blueprints. The specifications cover the knowledge representation layer (USO), the inter‑agent communication protocol (SCI‑ACP), the internal design of a prototypical domain agent, the scalability and computational complexity of the overall system, and the implementation stack envisioned for a first full‑scale prototype. No empirical implementation or training is reported here; rather, the chapter details the engineering decisions required to realise SAOA in practice.

5.1 Unified Scientific Ontology (USO)

The USO provides the canonical vocabulary and structural constraints that guarantee semantic interoperability among all agents and the orchestrator. Following the resolution of the OWL 2 DL contradiction (Gap 6), the USO is implemented as a labeled property graph schema, not an OWL ontology. The symbolic auditor, which already enforces physical constraints, is extended to enforce the ontological restrictions at run‑time.

Core classes. The USO defines a small set of foundational entity types, aligned conceptually with the Modern Science Ontology (ModSci) upper‑level categories:

· Material: Any substance or composite system under investigation (e.g., a specific crystal structure, a polymer blend, a drug formulation).
· Process: A physical, chemical, or computational transformation (e.g., synthesis, annealing, DFT relaxation, MD simulation).
· Property: A measurable or calculable attribute of a Material or the outcome of a Process, carrying a numerical value, a unit, and a standard uncertainty.
· Equation: A mathematical relationship that links Properties or constrains a Process.
· Experiment: A record of an empirical or simulated measurement, linking a Process to the resulting Properties and the agents that performed it.

Relations. The permitted relations are drawn from a controlled vocabulary:

Relation Domain Range Meaning
hasProperty Material, Process Property Subject possesses the property
causes Process Property Process outputs the property
measuredBy Property Experiment Property was determined by experiment
derivedFrom Material, Property Material, Property Indicates provenance or computational lineage
hasUnit Property Unit (qudt:Unit reference)
hasUncertainty Property Uncertainty Standard uncertainty value

Every Property node carries mandatory attributes: numericalValue (xsd:decimal), unit (a QUDT unit URI), and standardUncertainty. This makes all quantitative claims dimensionally checkable and statistically combinable.

Constraint enforcement. The symbolic auditor, before accepting any node, verifies that its incoming and outgoing edges conform to the domain‑range restrictions above, that all referenced units are dimensionally consistent, and that no duplicate Property nodes exist for the same entity (unless they are explicitly flagged as alternative hypotheses). This programmatic enforcement substitutes for an OWL reasoner and is fully coherent with the Neo4j property‑graph storage.

Serialisation. The USO graph is persisted in a Neo4j database and serialised for interchange using JSON‑LD with a context mapping that references ModSci classes. This enables export to institutional knowledge graphs, such as the Open Research Knowledge Graph, without requiring OWL reasoning at query time.

5.2 Science Community Inter‑Agent Communication Protocol (SCI‑ACP)

SCI‑ACP is a JSON‑based message protocol that structures all interactions among agents, the orchestrator, and the grounding layer. It defines exactly eleven message types, each with a fixed schema.

Message catalogue.

Type Purpose Key Payload Fields
Query Request computation or information req_id, concept, parameters
Hypothesis Propose a scientific claim hypo_id, claim, confidence, justification
Evidence Supply supporting data hypo_ref, data, provenance
Refutation Challenge a claim hypo_ref, counterargument, severity
RequestSimulation Invoke the Shared World Model system_spec, protocol
Result Return simulation/tool output req_ref, output, unit, uncertainty
ConflictNotification Initiate the CRP node_i, node_j, delta (precomputed)
ArbitrationRequest Ask for referee evaluation conflict_id, nodes
ArbitrationVerdict Referee decision conflict_id, accepted_node, rationale
StrategyProposal RSA → orchestrator goal goal_vector, mode, parameters
DistillationMessage Trigger knowledge transfer source, target, concept, data_ref

All messages carry a sender_id, a timestamp, and a correlation_id that links them to the originating task or conflict. The protocol follows a publish‑subscribe pattern over a message bus (e.g., Apache Kafka or Redis Streams) for decoupled scalability.

Interoperability. SCI‑ACP message types can be mapped to the emerging Model Context Protocol (MCP) specification by treating each message as a tool‑use invocation. An adapter layer translates between SCI‑ACP and MCP, enabling external institutional agents to join the SAOA community using standardised interfaces.

5.3 Domain Agent Blueprint

Every domain agent in SAOA is a container that combines an LLM backbone, domain‑specific tools, a local retrieval index, and a strict output protocol. The Materials Agent serves as the canonical example.

Foundational Layer. The agent’s language backbone is a 13B‑parameter decoder‑only model fine‑tuned on a corpus of 120,000 structured‑to‑natural‑language pairs derived from the Materials Project, AFLOW, and OQMD databases. Each training instance pairs a JSON document (crystal structure, band gap, formation energy, elastic moduli) with an abstract‑style natural language description, so that the model learns to ground its linguistic outputs in quantitative data.

Contextual Layer. A retrieval‑augmented generation (RAG) module maintains a dense vector index over the same databases. For an incoming task, the agent embeds the query, retrieves the top‑k = 5 entries, and re‑ranks them with a cross‑encoder. The retrieved entries are injected into the LLM prompt as structured context.

Operational Layer. Tool use is implemented via a ReAct‑style loop: the agent can issue database queries (e.g., “find materials with band gap between 1.0 and 2.0 eV”), invoke property calculators (Pymatgen, ASE), or launch simulation wrappers (Quantum ESPRESSO for DFT, LAMMPS for molecular dynamics). All tool outputs are formatted as USO‑compliant Property nodes.

Generative fallback. When a database query returns no entry for a requested material, the agent triggers a Crystal Diffusion Variational Autoencoder (CDVAE) to generate a novel candidate structure. The generated structure is automatically routed to the Physics Agent for DFT relaxation and property calculation. If the CDVAE itself fails (mode collapse), a negative‑result node is added to the USO graph, recording the absence of a known structure for the query.

Confidence convention. Claims backed by a direct database hit are tagged with confidence 1.0; CDVAE‑predicted properties are tagged with confidence 0.6. These values serve as the priors for the Beta capability updates in the orchestrator.

5.4 Scalability and Computational Complexity

The theoretical complexity of SAOA’s principal components is analysed here. The numbers are derived from the algorithms, not from empirical profiling.

Shapley value estimation. With m = 100 permutations and n agents, the estimation cost is O(m \cdot n), which is linear in the number of agents. For n \le 20 the overhead is negligible; for larger agent populations, stratified permutation sampling over importance‑weighted coalitions can keep the cost below 5 % of the total computational budget per episode.

MDP planning. Exact MCTS over the full action space is avoided by restricting the lookahead to depth 3 and using progressive widening. The state is compressed to a fixed 256‑dimensional embedding by the GAT encoder, so the cost of a forward simulation is dominated by the GAT forward pass and the agent response time, not by the state representation.

Verification. The symbolic auditor operates in O(|E| + |V|) time per proposed node, where E is the set of equations or constraints checked; this is negligible. The neural critic’s forward pass (a 7B‑parameter mixture‑of‑experts model) occupies approximately 50 ms on an A100 GPU. For high‑throughput experiments, multiple proposals can be batched, reducing the per‑proposal latency.

Conflict resolution simulations. Shared World Model invocations (DFT, MD) are the most expensive steps. They are placed in a priority queue and executed asynchronously on dedicated high‑performance computing nodes. Conflicts that can be resolved by a high‑competence referee agent do not incur simulation cost.

Knowledge distillation. LoRA fine‑tuning is performed on a separate GPU node and does not block the main discovery loop. The distributional compatibility check requires only m = 10 forward passes over small batches, adding negligible overhead.

Communication. The SCI‑ACP message bus is horizontally scalable; the number of messages per episode is proportional to the number of tasks, and the system can be deployed across a Ray cluster with hundreds of worker nodes.

5.5 Implementation Stack

A reference implementation of SAOA is envisioned using the following technologies, chosen for their maturity and interoperability:

· Distributed execution: Ray, for managing agent life‑cycles and parallel task execution.
· MDP environment: Gymnasium, with a custom environment class that wraps the orchestrator.
· Bayesian inference: Pyro, for Beta posterior maintenance and Thompson sampling.
· Knowledge graph: Neo4j, with the official Python driver; JSON‑LD serialisation via PyLD.
· GNN encoder: PyTorch Geometric, implementing a 3‑layer Graph Attention Network.
· Neural critic: A 7B‑parameter transformer (e.g., Llama‑3‑based) with LoRA‑trained expert heads, managed via the Hugging Face peft library.
· Communication bus: Redis Streams or Apache Kafka, with a thin SCI‑ACP adapter layer.
· RAG: Sentence‑Transformers for dense retrieval, Chroma or FAISS for the vector store.
· Simulation wrappers: Custom Python interfaces to Quantum ESPRESSO, LAMMPS, and Pymatgen.

All components are containerised (Docker) and orchestrated via Kubernetes for reproducibility across cloud and HPC environments.

