Chapter 7 – Discussion and Future Work

7.1 Limitations

No architecture, however carefully designed, is without limitations. The following are the principal bounds on SAOA’s applicability and robustness.

Domain ontology coverage. The USO is currently specialised for materials science and chemistry. Extending it to biology, social science, or climate science would require additional concept taxonomies and relation types. While the modular design permits such extensions, the effort would be substantial and the resulting ontologies would need their own domain‑specific auditing rules.

Simulation dependence for arbitration. The Conflict Resolution Protocol’s escalation to the Shared World Model assumes the availability of a high‑fidelity physics simulation that can adjudicate contradictory claims. For truly novel phenomena—where the relevant physical theory is itself under investigation—no such ground truth exists. In those cases, the system falls back to the highest‑confidence agent, which carries a risk of premature consensus on an incorrect hypothesis.

Adversarial agents. The Bayesian capability model, the Shapley credit scheme, and the verification layer all assume that agents are cooperative and non‑strategic. A malicious agent could, in principle, game its Beta posterior by selectively succeeding on easy tasks and failing only when detection is unlikely. Robust reputation schemes and behavioural audits would be required before SAOA could be deployed in an open, multi‑institutional setting where some agents might not be fully trusted.

Scalability of the knowledge graph traversal. As the USO graph grows to millions of nodes, the symbolic auditor’s checks and the GAT encoder must scale accordingly. This thesis has proposed caching strategies and depth‑limited MCTS, but a full implementation on petabyte‑scale knowledge bases would require approximate nearest‑neighbour methods for the GNN and incremental auditing rather than full‑graph passes.

Initial prior selection. The Beta priors are initialised with \alpha = 1, \beta = 1 (uniform) for every agent on every new concept. This is a principled default, but in domains where some agents are known a priori to have domain‑specific strengths—even without empirical data—more informative priors could accelerate the early stages of discovery. Incorporating such priors without compromising the system’s adaptivity is a subtle problem.

7.2 Future Work

Several axes of extension and refinement emerge naturally from the limitations and from the broader literature.

Causal discovery integration. The current task DAG captures dependency but not causation. Replacing it with a causal graph—and equipping the RSA with the ability to propose interventions—would align SAOA with Pearl’s causal hierarchy and enable it to perform active experimentation rather than purely observational inference.

Inter‑institutional federation. The SCI‑ACP protocol and the USO are designed to enable external agents to join the community. Future work could extend this to a federated setting where multiple SAOA instances, each running at a different institution, share distilled knowledge and aggregated capability statistics via differential privacy, thus preserving data confidentiality while enabling collective learning.

Human‑in‑the‑loop alignment. A scientist override channel—where a human expert can veto a verification decision, adjust the critic threshold \tau, or inject a domain constraint—would make SAOA a powerful assistant rather than a replacement for human judgment. Inverse reward design or preference‑based learning could be used to infer the scientist’s implicit objectives from these interventions.

Meta‑learning of the USO distance weights. The current CRP weight parameters w_q and w_s are proposed to be learned from a held‑out set of known conflicts. More ambitiously, they could be adapted online using Bayesian optimisation or meta‑gradients, allowing the system to become progressively better at distinguishing genuine conflicts from noise.

Ethical safeguards and dual‑use filtering. As SAOA becomes capable of generating novel chemical compounds and synthetic pathways, the risk of producing dangerous or weaponisable outputs grows. A third verification tier—explicitly trained to detect dual‑use proposals based on frameworks such as the Australia Group guidelines—would be a natural addition to the grounding layer.

Continuous capability shaping. The current Beta model updates only upon verification, which can be sparse. Incorporating intermediate process rewards (e.g., “the proposed calculation was dimensionally sound even if the final result was wrong”) could provide a richer signal and accelerate capability convergence.

7.3 Broader Impact

If realised, SAOA has the potential to transform how scientific discovery is conducted in computationally intensive fields. By automating the hypothesise‑test‑refute cycle with formal guarantees of physical soundness, the system could accelerate materials discovery, drug development, and climate modelling by orders of magnitude. Equally importantly, the architecture’s emphasis on verification, reproducibility, and fair credit addresses core concerns about trustworthiness and accountability in AI‑aided science.

The open release of the USO schema and the SCI‑ACP specification is intended to foster an ecosystem of interoperable, plug‑and‑play scientific agents, much as standardised benchmarks and APIs have catalysed progress in other areas of machine learning. By lowering the barrier to entry for domain experts who wish to contribute an agent, SAOA could democratise the construction of large‑scale scientific AI communities.

At the same time, the societal implications must be managed carefully. An autonomous discovery system, even with verification safeguards, could inadvertently generate pathways to hazardous materials or environmentally destructive processes. The ethical safeguards sketched above are not mere aspirational additions; they are an essential component for responsible deployment. The thesis advocates that no SAOA instance be connected to a physical laboratory automation loop without a thorough safety review and a mandatory human veto on any proposal that crosses a predefined risk threshold.

Ultimately, SAOA represents a step toward a future in which artificial scientific intelligence augments human creativity rather than replaces it—a future in which the bottleneck on discovery shifts from the speed of trial and error to the quality of the questions we dare to ask.


