Appendix C – Formal Statements and Proofs

C.1 Lemma 1 (Anti‑Free‑Riding Property)

Statement. Let the coalition utility function be v(S) = \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}_S} q(v), where \mathcal{V}_S is the set of verified nodes whose provenance trace contains only agents from S. If an agent i is such that for all subsets S \subseteq \mathcal{A} \setminus \{i\}, \mathcal{V}_{S \cup \{i\}} = \mathcal{V}_S (i.e., adding agent i does not result in any new verified node), then the Shapley value \phi_i = 0.

Proof. By definition, the Shapley value is

\phi_i = \sum_{S \subseteq \mathcal{A} \setminus \{i\}} \frac{|S|!\,(n-|S|-1)!}{n!} \bigl[ v(S \cup \{i\}) - v(S) \bigr].

For each S, v(S \cup \{i\}) - v(S) = \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}_{S \cup \{i\}}} q(v) - \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}_S} q(v). Since \mathcal{V}_{S \cup \{i\}} = \mathcal{V}_S by hypothesis, the difference is zero. Hence every term in the sum is zero, and \phi_i = 0. This is precisely the dummy player axiom of the Shapley value. ∎

C.2 Regret Bound for Factored Thompson Sampling

Statement (informal). Under the factored Thompson sampling rule of Section 4.2, operating on a sparse coordination graph with maximum degree d \ll n and K concepts, the expected cumulative regret over T task assignments is bounded by O(\sqrt{T} \cdot \text{poly}(d, K)).

Sketch of reduction. The task assignment problem with coordination graph C can be viewed as a contextual bandit where the reward for selecting agent i depends on the context (the upstream agent outcomes). The effective competence \tilde{c}_{i,k} is a stochastic estimate of the true success probability \theta_{i,k} minus a known deterministic penalty that depends on the current posterior means of upstream agents. Since the posterior means converge to the true means under Bayesian updating, the penalty term becomes a fixed scaling that does not affect the relative ordering in the limit.

Verstraeten et al. (2020, Theorem 1) provide a regret bound for factored Thompson sampling on a graph where the reward of each arm depends on the contexts of its neighbours. Our coordination graph matches their model: the success of agent i depends on the reliability of its upstream neighbours. Their proof, which relies on the properties of the Beta‑Bernoulli conjugate update and the sub‑Gaussian nature of the reward distribution, carries over directly. The additive penalty merely shifts the expected reward by a deterministic amount, which does not alter the stochastic exploration dynamics.

Thus, with probability at least 1 - \delta, expected regret is \tilde{O}(\sqrt{d\,K\,T}), where the poly(d,K) factor hides logarithmic terms and dependencies on the spectral gap of the graph. ∎

