Appendix F – Baseline Descriptions and Evaluation Environment

F.1 Static Orchestrator

A hand‑crafted mapping from task concept to agent. For example, all band‑gap tasks are routed to the Physics Agent, all synthesis tasks to the Materials Agent. No Bayesian tracking, no conflict resolution beyond accepting the most recent claim, no verification gate, no knowledge distillation. This represents the simplest rule‑based multi‑agent system.

F.2 Thompson‑only Dispatcher

Uses per‑concept Thompson sampling to assign each task independently, without a coordination graph. Conflicts are resolved by selecting the claim from the agent with the highest Beta mean. Verification is limited to a deterministic unit‑check only. No MDP planning, no Shapley credit, no distillation. This isolates the effect of factored vs. independent Thompson sampling and the additional SAOA components.

F.3 Hierarchical RL Baseline

A two‑tier reinforcement learning orchestrator inspired by recent multi‑agent scientific systems. A high‑level policy decomposes a discovery goal into subtasks and dispatches them to agents via a learned policy. The state space includes task type and recent agent success rates but not a full knowledge graph. Verification is a soft check (threshold on tool output). Credit assignment is team‑based (equal split). This baseline tests the benefit of SAOA’s richer state representation, rigorous verification, and fair credit scheme.

F.4 Evaluation Environment Configuration

· Agent pool: five agents: Materials Agent, Physics Agent, Chemistry Agent, Statistics Agent, and a Literature Agent (retrieval‑only).
· Task budgets: Thermoelectric discovery capped at 500 agent calls; PharmaBench at 300; RetroBench at 400.
· Computational cost model: each agent call incurs a virtual cost based on estimated GPU hours (1 unit for LLM call, 5 for DFT, 2 for MD).
· Metrics computed per episode, averaged over 20 independent runs with different random seeds.
· All external API responses are recorded via the snapshot protocol; LLM calls in deterministic replay mode are served from cache.

